 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board, delivered over self-hosted WebSocket connections that the server operates directly, without a third-party realtime vendor.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice. The change store keeps replayable revisions and publishes them reliably to connected sessions. Delivery owns WebSocket session lifecycle, including connection authorization and reconnect handling. Browsers detect disconnects and run catch-up logic against the change store's revision history after reconnecting. This approach favors fast updates and no recurring realtime-vendor fee over owning connection capacity and reconnect handling directly.
 
